Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.359 --> 00:00:07.590 align:start position:0%
 
a  21st century mine no elevator shafts  no pickaxes just  stacks and stacks of whirring computers  they're overheating circuit boards  naturally cooled by the chilly canadian  air  bit farms is a cryptocurrency mining  company  it has five of these sites in quebec its  president  jeffrey morphy says their machines  recently crossed a mind-boggling  milestone we are currently uh  generating over 1x a hash of production  one exahash exa being a one  followed by 18 zeros the number of  calculations they make  every second all searching for the next  hash number the first miner to find it  gets rewarded with  six and a quarter fresh bitcoins quebec  has quickly become a global hub for the  crypto mining industry  with around 80 companies now based in  the province  all fanned by the dizzying rise in the  price of bitcoin  as bitcoin prices started up in october  of last year  uh from 10 000 to 12 000 to 15  to 20 000 us dollars per bitcoin  we were ecstatic and then when it moved  through 30 and moved through 40 and  moved through 50  the margins just got stronger and  stronger  this modern virtual gold rush is all  made possible by what is known as  blockchain technology and how it  organizes  and secures transaction data  unlike a conventional database a  blockchain is replicated on  all users computers across a distributed  network a series of encrypted data  blocks  are arranged in a sequential chain a  blockchain  a new block of data containing new  transactions is added to the end of the  chain  every 10 minutes or so just like new  lines on a ledger  for security you can't change the old  blocks you can only add  new ones and even more importantly when  any bitcoin gets bought and sold  the data must be replicated on the  majority of the network  that helps to prevent hacking and fraud  miners verify the ever more complex  history of transactions  and get rewarded with new bitcoins  it's a pure bubble right it has no  intrinsic value  there i personally think bitcoin is a  long-term hedge  if you really understand bitcoin  just like the alberta oil sands the  market value of what you're digging for  must outweigh the cost of mining it  morphy says it currently costs them more  than  seven thousand bucks to mine one coin  but there's a finite number of bitcoins  left to mine  and over time the system makes them more  and more difficult  to find a recent estimate says the  industry worldwide uses as much energy  as sweden a cambridge university study  found that more than half of the mining  is powered by fossil fuels morphy says  that is why quebec with its low-cost  hydro power  makes greener sense our input in terms  of electricity our  largest input is done with renewable  energy  and then our output being heat we're  looking for  value chain opportunities there where we  can actually sell  or use our heat as a byproduct for other  businesses  one idea in the works is to invite a  tomato greenhouse farm to set up right  beside  the bitcoin farm but for now all that  bitcoin  heat is going unused  the growing field of blockchain is about  much more  than cryptocurrency just ask alex  tapscott  co-author of the book blockchain  revolution so what blockchain basically  enables is digital scarcity a way to  move  a unique digital asset over the internet  peer-to-peer to somebody else without  the need for  a traditional third party like a bank or  a government and that might seem like a  simple innovation but it's profound  profound because the system by its  design will  securely and automatically verify a  transaction  or contract you could theoretically  write a will or buy a house without a  lawyer land title registries are very  antiquated  mostly paper-based by moving them all  into a blockchain we could  have certainty that when you're buying a  home you know that the  occupant is the owner right put the  deposit money into you know  a digital escrow inside of a smart  contract  wait for the conditions of the  transaction to clear  at which point the transaction would  become irrevocable just written in code  how about an election without an army of  officials  you know vote is an asset you uh when  you vote once you  you want to make sure you can't vote  again and so it's useful even for  uh democracy and other government  applications  there are different types of blockchains  for different uses  one of the most popular being ethereum  co-founded by russian canadian vitalik  buterin  ethereum supports smart contracts and  the  ether currency for those who want to  trade in cryptocurrencies but don't want  the risk there are so called stable  coins  usually pegged to the u.s dollar  something that could benefit people  in developing nations you don't have  access to a bank account your local  currency is being inflated away you've  got to deal with the corrupt government  this is a godsend and we're starting to  see it get picked up  quite strongly in those parts of the  world despite the energy being used to  mine cryptocurrencies  this technology could actually be put to  work in tackling  climate change  pedro bis is the head of research at the  blockchain and  climate institute a think tank set up to  help governments and organizations  harness the power of blockchain we look  at the  financial sector as a whole and of the  green or sustainable financial products  by far green bonds stand out from the  crowd  and it could be one of those financial  instruments that really helps to  mobilize the  trillions of dollars that we need to  tackle to help tackle climate change  bis says blockchain could make green  bonds far more efficient and most  importantly  transparent for example satellites could  be used to track  tree planting efforts the bond buyer and  seller could instantly see the specific  trees they've planted and the current  size of the carbon offset  without the need for expensive site  surveys  it could encourage even the smallest of  farmers to get involved  really what we need is not just this tax  or or or labels of green what we need is  impact  what we need is measurable action that  we say oh yeah we remove this amount of  co2  we remove this amount of you know  energy consumption  a pilot project in ecuador sells  chocolate bars online  a blockchain token in the wrapper lets  you help farmers plant a new cocoa tree  which you can then track online  another green benefit of blockchain  could be making power grids  more efficient where anyone with a solar  panel could become a  prosumer selling energy not just to the  utility  but to anyone else on the grid  data is fundamental for this transition  and that's why you know there's been  this rollout for smart meters you know  and it's amazing how people to  understand that with our smart meters we  are not going to get a smart grid  and without a smart grid we are not  going to get a green you know  sustainable grid  i got solar panels because i had all  this roof space we have all the sun  i mean look at this this is fabulous  the aptly named daniel power joined the  brooklyn microgrid  pilot project hoping his bookstore could  go green  and so i will buy from my neighbors and  that are  prosumers i might buy for myself i don't  know  but regulation has lagged behind the  technology  and three years on the pandemic has  stalled the rollout  for now but after the approval from  the utilities commission to take it a  step further  nothing never happened that's when kobet  hit another small idea to plant  cedar trees in lebanon got cut down when  the lebanese economy collapsed  but tapscott believes these growing  pains are to be expected  and can be overcome if decision-makers  wrap their heads around  the complexities but also government  could be a model user of this technology  a lot of the best applications of  blockchain for example central bank  digital currencies  um you know digital health records land  titles and so forth  are things that the government could  benefit a lot from embracing so  um i'm pretty optimistic i've been  pretty pleased with what i've seen so  far and  i certainly hope it continues  blockchain's potential is huge  the biggest hurdle to it being realized  could be how hard it is  to grasp but then again how many of us  know what goes on under the hood of our  car  or inside our smartphone

